|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_reorder_derivatives_
    Among the functions below, which is the
    zeroth, first, and second derivative?
    (I.e., which is $f$, $f'$, and $f''$,
    assuming that relationship exists.)

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order.svg

    |> Solution

        The reodered sequence,
        from zeroth to second derivative,
        is:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_answer.svg

        Referring to this rearranged sequence,
        the first graph, for example,
        has a slope that starts at $\sim\!-1$ and
        ends at $\sim\!1$, while those are the
        values at which 
        the second graph
        starts and ends:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_first_pair_v2.svg

        Likewise, the second graph has slope
        close to $0$ at either end, and some
        slope near $1.5$ or $2$ towards the middle,
        matching the _values_ of the third graph:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_second_pair_v2.svg

        ...and none of this coincidentally, since the
        third graph is the derivative of the second, is the
        derivative of the first!

        !! |> ImageRight
        !!     src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_cloud.svg
        !!     offset_y=-0.1em
        !!     offset_x= -0.5em

